# Authentication

Every request to Wyrd authenticates as a **principal** — a typed caller with a
UUID and a kind: `User`, `Service { card_ref }`, or `Agent { card_ref }`. Service
and Agent principals are **bound to a registered card**, so calls from deployed
services and autonomous agents attribute to durable records, not to anonymous
keys.

Runtime calls carry a Bearer JWT in the **`X-Wyrd-Access-Token`** header. Wyrd does
not read the application-owned `Authorization` header for its own identity.

<Aside title="The shape of every credential flow" data-variant="wyrd">

**Issue** a long-lived bootstrap credential once → **exchange** it at
`POST /auth/token` for a short-lived access JWT (+ refresh token) → **authorize**
every subsequent call with that access JWT.

</Aside>

## Three ways to authenticate

| Credential | Principal | Use it for |
|---|---|---|
| **API key** | Service / Agent | Deployed services and agents that hold a static bootstrap secret. |
| **Human SSO** | User | Interactive logins through any OIDC IdP (Keycloak, Okta, …). |
| **Workload jwt-bearer** | Service / Agent | K8s pods, SPIFFE workloads, or CI runners that already hold a platform-issued OIDC JWT — no shared secret. |

Default TTLs: access token **15 min**, refresh token **30 days** (single-use
rotation for Service/Agent), API key **365 days**. Access tokens are EdDSA JWTs;
revocation is TTL-bounded in the current release.

## API key (services & agents)

An admin holding `service_accounts:write` issues a key for a registered Service or
Agent card. The plaintext key is returned **once** — store it in your secret store.

```bash
wyrd auth issue-key prod/Service/billing@1.0.0
```

The canonical CardRef text is `{space}/{kind}/{name}@{version}[#{uid}]`; the same
command shape works for an `Agent` card. At runtime, the workload exchanges the key
for tokens:

```http
POST /auth/token
Content-Type: application/json

{ "grant_type": "wyrd_api_key", "api_key": "wyrd_sk_..." }
```

The response carries `access_token`, `refresh_token`, `token_type`, and
`expires_at`. Send the access token in `X-Wyrd-Access-Token` on every call. The
bound `card_ref` lives inside the JWT claim, not on the wire envelope.

**Rotation.** Issue a new key, redeploy it alongside the old one, and let the old
key lapse at its TTL — no request fails mid-rotation. Reserve
`wyrd principal revoke <principal-id>` for a compromised credential; it is
immediate and severs every credential the principal holds.

## Human SSO (OIDC)

Direct the user to `GET /auth/login?issuer=<issuer>`. Wyrd redirects to the IdP;
after login the IdP returns to `/auth/callback?code=...&state=...`, which completes
the exchange and returns a User JWT and refresh token. A programmatic client that
captures `code`/`state` out of band can complete the exchange itself with
`grant_type: authorization_code`. The authorization code is single-use — use one
path or the other. MFA is handled entirely at the IdP.

## Workload jwt-bearer (federation)

A workload that already holds a platform-issued OIDC JWT exchanges it directly:

```http
POST /auth/token
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "grant_type": "urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:jwt-bearer",
  "assertion": "<platform-jwt>",
  "tenant": "acme"
}
```

The server verifies the JWT against the trusted issuer's JWKS, looks up the
`[[workload_bindings]]` entry matching `(issuer, subject)`, and returns a Service or
Agent JWT for the bound card. This is the recommended credential for Kubernetes,
SPIFFE, and CI — no shared secret leaves the platform.

## Configuring OIDC

Self-hosted Wyrd resolves trusted issuers and workload bindings from `server.toml`
at boot (single tenant per deployment). Each `[[trusted_issuers]]` entry declares
one provider; each `[[workload_bindings]]` entry maps a verified external subject
to a registered card — **the server never trusts a card identity from the token
itself**.

```toml
[auth]
tenant_slug = "acme"

[[trusted_issuers]]
issuer            = "https://idp.acme.com/realms/acme"
client_id         = "wyrd"
expected_audience = "wyrd"
client_auth       = { secret_post = "change-me" }
principal_kind    = "human"
default_roles     = ["viewer"]
claim_mapping     = { subject = "sub", email = "email", groups = "realm_access.roles" }
group_role_map    = { "data-science" = ["data_science"], "ml-platform" = ["admin"] }

[[workload_bindings]]
issuer  = "https://idp.acme.com/realms/workload"
subject = "system:serviceaccount:ml:inference-svc"
kind    = "service"
name    = "inference-svc"
space   = "prod"
version = "1.0.0"
```

`client_auth` accepts `"public"` (PKCE), `"private_key_jwt"`, or an inline
`{ secret_basic = "…" }` / `{ secret_post = "…" }`; secrets load as `SecretString`
and never print. Issuer and binding changes require a config edit and restart.

## Current limits

All tokens are verified as **JWTs** — opaque tokens and RFC 7662 introspection are
not supported. MFA, SCIM provisioning, and Single Logout are delegated to or out of
scope for the IdP. User refresh-token rotation and the RFC 8693 token-exchange
(act-as delegation) grant are not enabled in the current release — see the
[Roadmap](/roadmap/).

